with that symbol. The phrase \"a required symbol\" probably means if the node's symbol is in the required_symbols list, then we must keep it. Otherwise why have required_symbols? The instruction: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So that means after removal, for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol exists. So we cannot remove the last node of a required symbol. But we can remove a node that has a required symbol if there is another node with that symbol. So \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be ambiguous, but the tentatively remove condition implies we can remove if representation remains. So we'll implement: we can remove a node if (1) it's not root (id != \"root\"), (2) it's removable flag True, (3) it has no retained children (i.e., all its children have been removed already), (4) after removing it, for each symbol in required_symbols, there exists at least one retained node with that symbol.\n\nWe also need to consider that the node's parent may have other children that are retained, so removing this node does not affect parent's leaf status until later.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build a dict node_by_id.\n- Build adjacency: children for each node (list of child IDs).\n- Keep a set of retained IDs initially all node IDs.\n- Remove root from removable consideration (it's not removable? root has removable false anyway).\n- We want to process nodes in decreasing depth, but depth ties broken by node ID (string). Since depth is integer, we can sort nodes by (-depth, id). That gives deepest first, then lexicographic for tie.\n- However, after we remove a node, its children are also removed? Wait: if we remove a node, do we also remove its descendants? The text says \"Tentatively remove a node\". If we remove a node, its children must also be removed because they are under it. The tree structure implies that if a node is removed, its entire subtree is removed. But the instruction \"Never remove a node with retained children\" suggests that we cannot remove a node that still has children in the retained set. So we can only remove leaf nodes. If we remove a leaf, its parent might later become leaf and be removed. So the removal of a node implicitly removes its subtree only if we remove the ancestor. But if we remove a leaf, the ancestor stays. So we are only removing leaf nodes one by one, not whole subtrees. So we don't remove children when removing a node; only the node itself. That's plausible. But then \"never remove a node with retained children\" ensures we don't remove a node that has children still present. So we only remove leaves. So after removing a leaf, its parent might become leaf (if all its children are removed), and then can be considered later.\n\nThus we need to repeatedly consider nodes that are currently leaves and removable, and removed them if condition holds, until no more can be removed.\n\nWe need to produce removal order (list of IDs removed) and retained IDs sorted.\n\nImplementation: \n- Build tree.\n- Initialize retained = set(all node ids).\n- Initialize children_map (node_id -> list of child ids).\n- Initialize parent_map.\n- We'll maintain a set of leaf nodes (nodes with no children in retained set).\n- We'll use a priority queue or just repeatedly scan? Since depth order is important: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should prioritize deeper nodes among leaf candidates. We can process in decreasing depth order initially, but after removals, new leaves appear at shallower depths, so we need to consider them later. So we can maintain a max-heap keyed by (-depth, id) for nodes that are currently leaves and removable, and not root. But we also need to check condition (required symbols). We'll pop the deepest, test removal, if valid, remove it, update leaf status of its parent, and maybe add parent to heap if it becomes leaf and removable. Continue until heap empty.\n\n- For checking required symbols: keep a counter of how many retained nodes per symbol? We need to ensure after removal, for each required symbol, count>=1. We can maintain a dict symbol_count (retained nodes per symbol). When removing a node, decrement its symbol count; if any required symbol becomes 0, we cannot remove. So we need to check before removal: after removal, count[symbol]-1 >=1 for each required symbol? Actually only if that symbol is required and the node is the only one carrying it. So we can check: if node.symbol in required_symbols and symbol_count[node.symbol] == 1, then cannot remove. Also we must ensure the node is removable and not root.\n\n- We must also consider that nodes with removable=False are never removed.\n\nEdge cases: If some required symbol is not present at all initially, then infeasible -> raise ValueError. But the fixtures likely have them.\n\nAlso the root is \"root\" and its symbol \"root\". required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\". So root must be retained.\n\nWe'll implement.\n\nReturn format: we need to output JSON with implementation function. The function should return a dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". The retained_ids sorted (ascending, presumably string sort). removal_order as list in order of removal.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nWe need to ensure no mutations of case, and use only standard library.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that takes the payload dict.\n\nWe'll parse case[\"nodes\"], case[\"required_symbols\"].\n\nBuild nodes dict.\n\nValidate: all nodes have required fields? id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. depth integer? float? We'll assume int. removable bool.\n\nCheck that root exists and depth 0.\n\nIf any missing, raise ValueError.\n\nBuild children map.\n\nInitialize symbol_counts = defaultdict(int). For each node: symbol_counts[node.symbol] += 1.\n\nCheck that for each req in required_symbols, symbol_counts[req] >= 1.\n\nInitialize retained set.\n\nInitialize leaf candidates: for each node id, check if it has no children in retained (i.e., children list empty). Since initially all nodes retained, leaf nodes are those with no children (i.e., len(children_map[id])==0). But also consider that some nodes may have children that are not in retained? All are retained initially, so children list is from tree. So leaf: node with no children.\n\nWe'll maintain a heap (list) and use heapq module.\n\nHeap entries: (-depth, id). We'll only push nodes that are removable and not root, and are currently leaf (no children in retained). But we need to recompute leaf status as we remove. So we'll push when we identify a leaf candidate. Initially push all leaves that are removable and not root.\n\nThen while heap:\n  pop deepest (negative depth). depth = -neg_depth, id.\n  If id not in retained: continue (maybe removed earlier)\n  Check if it is still leaf: i.e., all its children are not in retained? Actually children may be removed already. So check if any child id is in retained. If any, skip (not leaf anymore). This can happen if we processed a node before its children were removed? But we only push leaves, and children removal might make a node leaf later, so we push after removal. However, it's possible that a node was pushed as leaf, but later some child becomes retained? No, we only remove nodes, never add. So once a node becomes leaf, it remains leaf. So we don't need to recheck, but safe to do.\n  Check if node is removable (True) and id != \"root\".\n  Check if removal preserves required symbols: if node.symbol in required_symbols and symbol_counts[node.symbol] == 1: cannot remove.\n  If all checks pass:\n    remove id from retained.\n    decrement symbol_counts[node.symbol].\n    Add id to removal_order list.\n    Now, parent may become leaf: if node.parent is not None, then parent node (id=node.parent) should be checked if it now has no children in retained. But we need to ensure that parent is still in retained. If parent is still retained, and all its children are now removed (i.e., for each child in children_map[parent], child not in retained), then parent becomes a new leaf. If parent is removable and not root, push it onto heap.\n  Continue.\n\nAfter loop, remaining retained nodes are the final set.\n\nSort retained_ids list.\n\nReturn {\"retained_ids\": sorted_retained, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nPotential issue: What about nodes that are not removable? They will never be removed. But they may be leaf initially. They will be skipped in heap because we only push removable. So they remain.\n\nAlso, need to consider that a node might be removable but not leaf initially; later when its children are removed, it becomes leaf. So we push it when parent is updated.\n\nEdge case: If a node has children but those children are all not removable? They will never be removed, so the node will never become leaf. That's fine.\n\nNow, we need to handle the case where there is a cycle? Not.\n\nWe'll write the code.\n\nAlso note: The payload includes _case_nonce, but we ignore.\n\nImplementation must be inside solve function.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable object. We'll use standard Python types.\n\nNow, produce the output as a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source as a string. Need to escape newlines, quotes, backslashes.\n\nWe'll write the code as a string.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1919,"completion_tokens":3805,"total_tokens":5724,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3085},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":127},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}